        {
          "id": "the_output_row_carries_its_keys_and_leaves_the_absent_ones_absent",
          "description": "PC-6's key columns on the same rows. The day type, fuel type and model year come from joins to the day and cohort relations on their ordinals; the SCC is COMPUTED as 22e8 + fuel x 1e6 + sourceType x 1e4 + roadType x 1e2 + process, giving 2201210411 for gasoline and 2205210411 for E85 -- the same two SCCs as the leaks slice with a different process suffix; and `regClassID` is absent, which the indicator reads as 1 while the same template on the present `emissionQuant` reads 0. What breaks if this is wrong: the comparator treats SCC as an identity column, so a hard-coded value would be right for the 82 gasoline rows and come back as 46 missing plus 46 extra keys for the E85 ones rather than as a wrong number. The absence cannot be pinned directly because `Assertion.expected` is a JSON number and a NaN actual fails every tolerance form (docs/esm-conventions.md 18.2).",
          "time_span": { "start": 0.0, "end": 0.0 },
          "assertions": [
            { "variable": "out_dayID", "time": 0.0, "expected": 2.0, "coords": { "output_rows": 41 } },
            { "variable": "out_dayID", "time": 0.0, "expected": 5.0, "coords": { "output_rows": 105 } },
            { "variable": "out_fuelTypeID", "time": 0.0, "expected": 1.0, "coords": { "output_rows": 41 } },
            { "variable": "out_fuelTypeID", "time": 0.0, "expected": 5.0, "coords": { "output_rows": 60 } },
            { "variable": "out_modelYearID", "time": 0.0, "expected": 1980.0, "coords": { "output_rows": 41 } },
            { "variable": "out_modelYearID", "time": 0.0, "expected": 2002.0, "coords": { "output_rows": 60 } },
            { "variable": "out_modelYearID", "time": 0.0, "expected": 1998.0, "coords": { "output_rows": 64 } },
            { "variable": "out_SCC", "time": 0.0, "expected": 2201210411.0, "coords": { "output_rows": 41 } },
            { "variable": "out_SCC", "time": 0.0, "expected": 2205210411.0, "coords": { "output_rows": 60 } },
            { "variable": "out_roadTypeID", "time": 0.0, "expected": 4.0, "coords": { "output_rows": 41 } },
            { "variable": "out_stateID", "time": 0.0, "expected": 26.0, "coords": { "output_rows": 41 } },
            { "variable": "absentRegClassIndicator", "time": 0.0, "expected": 1.0, "coords": { "output_rows": 41 } },
            { "variable": "presentQuantIndicator", "time": 0.0, "expected": 0.0, "coords": { "output_rows": 41 } }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "pc5_divides_by_the_day_types_real_days",
          "description": "PC-5's activity and its divisor, separated from the emission so that a failure attributes. `outSourceHours` is the activity relation's value at this row's (hourDayID, ageID) and `outNoOfRealDays` is 2 on the weekend rows and 5 on the weekday ones -- the same factor A7 multiplied in through `dayVMTFraction`. What breaks if this is wrong: applying the divisor in both places or in neither is a factor of 2 and 5 on every cell, and dropping it alone was measured on the oracle as a worst-cell error of 4.000e+00 (docs/evap-permeation.md 7.1).",
